 "fieldData": {
  "awb---faq-data": "<div data-rt-embed-type='true'><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><script> window.awbFAQ = {\"heading\": \"Card Authorization Questions, Answered\", \"items\": [{\"q\": \"What is a credit card authorization form?\", \"a\": \"A credit card authorization form is a signed permission that lets a business charge a customer's card, once or on a schedule. It names the card and the amount or terms, and the cardholder signs it. The business keeps it as proof the charge was approved.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do I create a credit card authorization form online?\", \"a\": \"Describe the charge to Emergent's <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-form-builder'>AI form builder</a>: who signs, what the card pays for, and how often. It builds the form with Stripe's card fields and an on-screen signature, plus a table that holds each signed record.\"}, {\"q\": \"Is it safe to fill out a credit card authorization form?\", \"a\": \"It is safe when the card number stays off paper and out of email. A cc authorization form PDF returned as an attachment leaves the full number in someone's inbox. On a form Emergent builds, the number goes into Stripe's card fields and the business sees only the last four digits. A purchase paid on the spot needs a checkout form instead.\"}, {\"q\": \"Should a credit card authorization form template ask for the security code?\", \"a\": \"No. Once a charge is approved, card security standards forbid storing the three- or four-digit code printed on the card. A CVV box on a template creates a copy nobody may keep. Leave it out of your credit card authorization form template and let the payment processor check the code when the card is entered.\"}, {\"q\": \"What is a payment authorization form?\", \"a\": \"A payment authorization form is the broader version: the customer approves a charge to a card or a debit from a bank account. Bank debits run on the ACH network, where each debit must be authorized by the account holder. The form states the amount, the timing, and how to cancel.\"}, {\"q\": \"Is a credit card form the same thing as an authorization form?\", \"a\": \"Usually not. A credit card form usually charges the card the moment the customer submits it, like a payment form at checkout. An authorization form records permission for charges that come later, such as a monthly retainer or a no-show fee. Many businesses use one of each.\"}, {\"q\": \"How long should a business keep a cc authorization form?\", \"a\": \"Keep each signed cc authorization form while you charge the card, then until the dispute window on the last charge closes. Card networks typically allow 120 days, counted from the service date for work booked ahead. That form is your proof of consent in a dispute. On a form built with Emergent, every signed record sits in a database you own, with no response caps.\"}, {\"q\": \"What does a sample credit card authorization form look like?\", \"a\": \"A sample credit card authorization form fits on one page: the cardholder's details, the card details, then a short authorization paragraph above the signature line. That paragraph matters most. It names the business and the amount, plus the schedule, such as $120 on the 1st of each month until the cardholder cancels in writing.\"}, {\"q\": \"Is a credit authorization form the same as a credit check authorization?\", \"a\": \"No. On a sales or service account, a credit authorization form means permission to charge a card. A credit check authorization is a separate document that lets a landlord or an employer pull your credit report. An employer must have that permission in writing before ordering the report.\"},{\"q\": \"When does a hotel ask for a credit card authorization form?\", \"a\": \"A hotel asks for one when someone pays for another guest's stay, such as a parent covering a student's room. The cardholder lists who is staying and states what the card covers, often room and tax only. Many hotels send it as a credit card authorization form PDF to fill in and email back.\"}]}; </script></div></div>"
 }
